===
title: Mill, On Liberty (1859), ch. II, closing recapitulation
source: Project Gutenberg eBook #34901, lines 2312-2335
===

# Main conclusion

[Free Opinion]: Freedom of opinion, and of its expression, is necessary to the mental well-being of mankind.
  {source: "We have now recognised the necessity to the mental well-being of mankind (on which all their other well-being depends) of freedom of opinion, and freedom of the expression of opinion, on four distinct grounds", locator: "para. 1"}

# Arguments

<Fallibility>: Silencing an opinion assumes an infallibility no one has.

(1) Any opinion compelled to silence may, for aught we can certainly know, be true.
    {source: "if any opinion is compelled to silence, that opinion may, for aught we can certainly know, be true", locator: "para. 2"}
(2) To deny that the silenced opinion may be true is to assume our own infallibility.
    {source: "To deny this is to assume our own infallibility.", locator: "para. 2"}
(3) We are not infallible.
    {implicit: true, reason: "needed for (1)-(2) to yield (4); Mill leaves it unstated here"}
----
(4) Silencing an opinion risks suppressing truth on an assumption we are not entitled to make.
  +> [Free Opinion]

<Partial Truth>: Even an erroneous opinion supplies the part of the truth the prevailing view lacks.

(1) A silenced opinion, though an error, may, and very commonly does, contain a portion of truth.
    {source: "though the silenced opinion be an error, it may, and very commonly does, contain a portion of truth", locator: "para. 3"}
(2) The prevailing opinion on any subject is rarely or never the whole truth.
    {source: "the general or prevailing opinion on any subject is rarely or never the whole truth", locator: "para. 3"}
(3) Only by the collision of adverse opinions does the remainder of the truth have any chance of being supplied.
    {source: "it is only by the collision of adverse opinions, that the remainder of the truth has any chance of being supplied", locator: "para. 3"}
----
(4) Silencing erroneous opinions removes the means of completing the truth.
  +> [Free Opinion]

<Dead Dogma>: Uncontested truth is held as prejudice.

(1) Unless a received opinion is suffered to be, and actually is, vigorously contested, most who receive it will hold it as a prejudice, with little comprehension of its rational grounds.
    {source: "unless it is suffered to be, and actually is, vigorously and earnestly contested, it will, by most of those who receive it, be held in the manner of a prejudice, with little comprehension or feeling of its rational grounds", locator: "para. 4"}
(2) Holding the truth as prejudice, without comprehension of its grounds, damages mental well-being.
    {implicit: true, reason: "links (1) to the thesis's 'mental well-being'"}
----
(3) Even a wholly true received opinion needs open contest.
  +> [Free Opinion]

<Lost Meaning>: Uncontested doctrine loses its meaning and its effect on conduct.

(1) Without contest, the meaning of the doctrine itself is in danger of being lost or enfeebled, and deprived of its vital effect on character and conduct.
    {source: "the meaning of the doctrine itself will be in danger of being lost, or enfeebled, and deprived of its vital effect on the character and conduct", locator: "para. 4"}
(2) A doctrine reduced to a mere formal profession prevents the growth of real conviction from reason or personal experience.
    {source: "the dogma becoming a mere formal profession, inefficacious for good, but cumbering the ground, and preventing the growth of any real and heartfelt conviction, from reason or personal experience", locator: "para. 4"}
----
(3) Suppressing contest destroys the living force of even true doctrine.
  +> [Free Opinion]
